"""Closed lexical grammar for the native shared serial processor.

This is an executable token grammar, not a body hash: whitespace and source
formatting do not affect it, while every operator, operand, branch and callback
that controls serial decoding is bound. A changed processor algorithm requires a
new reviewed grammar version; table rows remain separately source-derived.

Two grammar versions are accepted. Each is a complete token stream; neither is
selected by an ExifTool release label, only by the captured processor body.

* ``V1`` captures ``FoundTag``'s returned key and then, only when the
  ``SaveFormat`` / ``SaveBin`` options are set, stores
  ``$$et{TAG_EXTRA}{$key}{G6}`` (the read format) and
  ``$$et{TAG_EXTRA}{$key}{BinVal}`` (the raw field bytes).
* ``V0`` is the same algorithm without those two option-gated stores: it runs
  ``$count and $et->FoundTag($tagInfo, $val)`` and discards the result.

The V1-only statements write only ``TAG_EXTRA`` and change nothing the loop
reads afterwards (``$val``, ``%val``, ``$pos``, ``$count``, ``$format`` and the
table are untouched), and they execute only under two non-default options that
neither the descriptor nor the Rust serial engine models. Every other token --
selection, count evaluation, bounded reads, raw prior values, subdirectory
recursion, the ``$count``-guarded ``FoundTag`` call and state restoration -- is
identical. ``test_serial_processor_grammar.py`` pins that V0 is exactly V1 with
that one span replaced, so neither list can drift silently.
"""

